<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>6.5.5: Choosing Your Own Method to Factor Trinomials</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01668</md:content-id>
    <md:title>6.5.5: Choosing Your Own Method to Factor Trinomials</md:title>
    <md:uuid>378be803-613a-419e-9a8b-aac5cfb9f687</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<para>Work with a partner. For each trinomial, explain which method you would use to factor. When you have discussed your reasoning, factor the trinomial and compare solutions.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(
        x^2 − 14x − 32\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>trial and error; for example, \((x − 16)(x + 2)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(6r^2 + 30r + 36\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>the “\(ac\)” method; for example, \(6(r + 3)(r + 2)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \((x + 3)^2 − 9(x + 3) − 36\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>substitution; for example, \((x − 9)(x + 6)\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
